<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harmonia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harmonia was a goddess of Greek myth, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a> and the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmus">Cadmus</a>, founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. The gods attended their wedding, at which she received a necklace that brought misfortune to all who later owned it. In some traditions she and Cadmus ended their lives ruling the Illyrian Enchelei before being changed into serpents.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harmonia.jpg" alt="Harmonia" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allen Shue · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Harmonia_old_economy_village_pa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Harmonia derives from the Greek noun harmoníā, meaning means of joining, frame, covenant or agreement, which in turn comes from an adjective preserved only as the personal name Harmōn. In myth she is the goddess of harmony and concord. Her parentage varies between accounts. Most often she is the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a>, which makes her the sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a>, Phobos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eros">Eros</a>.</p><p>In other accounts she was born on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and the Pleiad Electra, and was the sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dardanus">Dardanus</a> and Iasion, who under the instruction of Zeus founded the mystic rites of that island. She was also closely associated with Aphrodite Pandemos, an aspect of Aphrodite that personified order and civic unity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Almost every account marries Harmonia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmus">Cadmus</a>, the mythical hero and founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. In the version set on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a> she was given to Cadmus, or carried off by him, after he was initiated into the island's mysteries. In the other version <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> gave her to him when he founded Thebes and finished eight years of servitude to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a> for killing a dragon sacred to the god. All the gods attended the wedding and gave gifts, most notably a peplos and a necklace wrought by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephaestus">Hephaestus</a>.</p><p>Common versions say <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephaestus">Hephaestus</a> made the necklace in anger at his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a>'s affair with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a>, and vowed to curse any children of that union. Other traditions credit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, Aphrodite or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmus">Cadmus</a>'s sister Europa with the gifts. Ancient passages describe the Necklace of Harmonia as beautifully wrought gold inlaid with jewels, typically emeralds. Hyginus blames not a necklace but a peplos dipped in crime, given by Hephaestus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>. Harmonia and Cadmus had Ino, Polydorus, Autonoë, Agave and Semele, and in some accounts Illyrius. Through Semele she was the grandmother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The necklace brought misfortune on all who wore it, chiefly the queens and princesses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. It passed through Pentheus to Jocasta, who killed herself on learning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oedipus">Oedipus</a>'s identity, and then to her son Polynices, who used it to bribe Eriphyle into urging her husband Amphiaraus to join the Seven against Thebes. After Amphiaraus died, Alcmaeon killed Eriphyle and took the necklace, and he in turn was killed. His sons dedicated it at the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> at Delphi, from where the Phocian general Phayllus stole it. His mistress wore it until her youngest son, seized by madness, burned down the house with her inside.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmus">Cadmus</a> was expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, Harmonia went with him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a>, where he became king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a>. He was turned into a serpent, and Harmonia begged the gods to share his fate. They took pity and changed her too, and the couple were sent to Elysium.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harmonia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Myth said Cadmus and <b>Harmonia</b> came to rule them after leaving Thebes, and that their descendants ruled the Illyrians.</blockquote><a href="/state/enchelei">Enchelei</a></li></ul></section>
